          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Critical Philosophy</strong><br>\nKant's most significant contribution to philosophy was his \"Critical Philosophy,\" which he began with the \"Critique of Pure Reason\" in 1781. In this book, Kant sought to reconcile the rationalist and empiricist philosophical traditions. He argued that our understanding of the physical world is shaped by our perceptions, which are structured by the innate categories of our minds.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ethics and Morality</strong><br>\nKant further explored ethics in his \"Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals\" published in 1785. He proposed the idea of a \"categorical imperative,\" which is a universal moral law applicable to all rational beings. According to Kant, actions are morally right if they can be universalized without contradiction and treat others as ends in themselves, not as means to an end.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Politics and Peace</strong><br>\nIn \"Perpetual Peace: A Philosophical Sketch\" (1795), Kant laid out his vision for a world where nations coexist peacefully through democracy and international cooperation. He believed in a federation of free states that would respect each other's sovereignty and citizens' rights, leading to lasting peace and a collective security.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
