          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence on Modern Thought</strong><br>\nKant's philosophy profoundly influenced various disciplines, including metaphysics, epistemology, ethics, and political theory. His work set the stage for the development of German idealism, influenced by thinkers such as Hegel, Schopenhauer, and Nietzsche, and remains central to contemporary debates in philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Late Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Kant continued to write and teach until old age and poor health slowed his productivity. He died on February 12, 1804, in his hometown of Königsberg. Today, Kant is remembered as one of the most important figures in modern philosophy. His ideas continue to resonate with various academic fields, including philosophy, political science, and law.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kant's Relevance in Education</strong><br>\nKant's thoughts on education were revolutionary for his time. He believed that education should not merely convey knowledge but also develop character. He was one of the early advocates for education's role in cultivating moral citizens, which aligns with the goals of modern educational systems worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
